Wi-Fi on this board
===================

The hardware works out of the box; the credentials are yours to add. Nothing
here is enabled by default, because a wpa_supplicant with no network block
fails at boot and would leave a permanently red systemctl status on an image
most people will run over ethernet.

To connect:

    wpa_passphrase 'YOUR-SSID' 'YOUR-PASSPHRASE' \
        > /etc/wpa_supplicant/wpa_supplicant-wlan0.conf
    chmod 600 /etc/wpa_supplicant/wpa_supplicant-wlan0.conf
    systemctl enable --now wpa_supplicant@wlan0

systemd-networkd picks it up from there - see
/etc/systemd/network/wlan.network, which is already installed and will DHCP
the interface once it associates. Ethernet stays preferred while both are up.

Note that wpa_passphrase writes the plaintext passphrase into the file as a
comment above the hashed PSK. Delete that line if it bothers you; the hash on
its own is enough to connect.

Set your country while you are in there:

    country=TW          # first line of the file, ISO 3166 alpha-2

It is not cosmetic. Without it the kernel stays on the world regulatory
domain "00", and 5 GHz behaves differently from what you expect - see below.


5 GHz, DFS, and channels you cannot see
---------------------------------------

If a 5 GHz network is missing from a scan while its 2.4 GHz twin shows up,
check which channel it is on before assuming anything is broken.

Channels 52-144 (5250-5330 and 5490-5730 MHz) are DFS: radar-sharing
channels, which a client may only listen on passively. Measured on this
board: with channels 100-144 enabled and three full scans, **nothing at all
was found anywhere in 5250-5730**, while APs on 5220, 5785 and 5825 came back
every time. The BCM4356 firmware does not appear to scan the DFS block for a
station, whatever the regulatory domain says about it.

The driver says as much if you ask it. Under `country TW`, a non-DFS channel
advertises what it can do and a DFS one advertises nothing but the hazard:

    * 5745 MHz [149]
      Maximum TX power: 20.0 dBm
      Channel widths: 20MHz HT40+ VHT80
    * 5540 MHz [108]
      Maximum TX power: 20.0 dBm
      Radar detection                     <- and no channel widths at all

So a router on channel 108 is invisible here. The fix is the router's, and it
costs nothing: move its 5 GHz radio to a non-DFS channel - 36, 40, 44, 48 at
the bottom, or 149, 153, 157, 161, 165 at the top.

Channel width is not the problem and does not have to be given up. A station
scans on the primary 20 MHz channel, so an 80 MHz AP is found - or not - by
its primary alone. Both non-DFS blocks take a full 80 MHz:

    36 / 40 / 44 / 48     primary 36,  centre 42   5150-5250, 23 dBm in TW
    149 / 153 / 157 / 161 primary 149, centre 155  5725-5850, 30 dBm in TW

The upper block is the better of the two here: same width, 7 dB more power
allowed, and no DFS anywhere near it. Channel 165 is 20 MHz only and cannot
be part of an 80 MHz block.

Two related things that are easy to trip over:

  * `iw reg set XX` does nothing while you are associated. An AP's beacon
    carries a country IE, and cfg80211 intersects that with your setting -
    `iw reg get` then reports "country 98", the intersection, and the channel
    list is whatever survived it. Stop wpa_supplicant first if you want to
    see what your own setting does.

  * Going straight from one country to another does not always take. The
    driver skips the update when the firmware already holds the code it is
    being handed, so the channel list is not rebuilt. `iw reg set 00` first,
    then the country you want.

What the hardware is
--------------------

An AP6356S module: a Broadcom BCM4356 on SDIO, at mmc2. It is driven by
mainline brcmfmac, not by Rockchip's bcmdhd, which the vendor defconfig
selects and which cannot work on this device tree - the reasoning is in
config/kernel-fragments/pikvm.config in the build tree.

Two things have to line up for the chip to come up, and both are already in
the image:

  * firmware      /usr/lib/firmware/brcm/brcmfmac4356-sdio.bin and .clm_blob,
                  from linux-firmware
  * per-board     /usr/lib/firmware/brcm/brcmfmac4356-sdio.AP6356S.txt, also
    NVRAM         from linux-firmware. brcmfmac finds it by the board-type
                  the device tree declares; kernel patch 0001 is what sets it.

If wlan0 never appears, that pair is the first thing to check:

    dmesg | grep brcmfmac

A "Direct firmware load for brcm/... failed" line names the file it wanted.
